      "evidence": [3, 4],
      "text": "Measurement techniques for cybersickness (CS) rely on three components: the input provided for measurement, the measurement technique itself, and the output generated."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [5],
      "text": "Inputs for measuring cybersickness (CS) can be questionnaire-based, physiological and biometric information, behavioral information, and content-based information."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [6, 7, 8],
      "text": "The measurement technique or evaluation process for cybersickness (CS) can be manual, automated, machine learning-based, or real-time evaluation while the experience is taking place."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [10],
      "text": "The output of cybersickness (CS) measurement can be binary classes, multiple classes, or a sickness score that is a number."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [15, 16, 17, 18, 19],
      "text": "Questionnaire-based input for measuring cybersickness (CS) is a common method that uses self-report questionnaires or scales, generally in the form of ratings, to rate users' discomfort or disorientation on a numerical scale."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [17, 20],
      "text": "Questionnaire-based cybersickness (CS) scales are easy to administer and help researchers or practitioners better understand the causes of cybersickness and identify potential interventions."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [17, 21, 22, 23],
      "text": "Questionnaire-based cybersickness (CS) scales are typically administered before or after an experience, can also be administered during the experience, with before and after being the most common timing and during being occasional."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [24, 25],
      "text": "The Simulator Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ) is one of the most extensively used questionnaires for measuring cybersickness (CS)."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [24, 26],
      "text": "The Simulator Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ) contains 16 items divided into three subscales: the oculomotor subscale, the disorientation subscale, and the nausea subscale."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [24, 27],
      "text": "In the Simulator Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ), participants rate the intensity of individual symptoms belonging to the oculomotor, disorientation, and nausea subscales on a scale from 0 to 3, where a higher score denotes more severe symptoms."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [24, 28],
      "text": "The Simulator Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ) was not originally designed for virtual reality (VR) but was adapted for use in the context of virtual reality."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [28],
      "text": "Questionnaires specifically designed for virtual reality (VR) include the Virtual Reality Sickness Questionnaire (VRSQ) and the Cyber Sickness Questionnaire (CSQ), which is good for evaluating virtual reality experiences with head-mounted displays (HMDs)."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [29],
      "text": "Shorter and more concise questionnaires used to assess specific conditions include the Misery Scale (MIS) and the Fast Motion Sickness Scale (FMS)."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [30, 31],
      "text": "Questionnaires used to measure susceptibility to cybersickness (CS) include the Motion History Questionnaire (MHQ), the Motion Sickness Susceptibility Questionnaire (MS-SQ), and the Visually Induced Motion Sickness Susceptibility Questionnaire (VIMS or VIMS-SQ)."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [32],
      "text": "Questionnaires commonly used to assess overall discomfort include the Nausea Scale and the Motion Sickness Assessment Questionnaire (MSAQ)."
    }
  ]
}

