{
  "facts": [
    {
      "evidence": [36, 37, 38, 39],
      "text": "Immersion in virtual reality (VR) means that the user's senses are blocked so that the user receives no input from the real-world environment and receives input only from the virtual world."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [4, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11, 14, 15],
      "text": "From a systems point of view, virtual reality (VR) systems are broadly categorized into non-immersive, semi-immersive, and fully immersive systems because market applications offer varying degrees of immersion rather than all being fully immersive."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [19, 21, 24, 33, 88],
      "text": "A non-immersive virtual reality (VR) system is a specific, limited class of computer application, not any computer application, that provides a computer-generated environment in which the user can control characters or activities while retaining control of the physical environment, and the virtual environment does not directly interact with the user."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [40, 41, 43, 44, 87],
      "text": "A non-immersive virtual reality (VR) application is a regular computer system or software that completely takes away the user's attention, so the user is psychologically engaged and may not experience the real world, although the user's eyes, ears, and other sensory organs remain open and the user is physically attached to the real world."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [25, 30, 31, 32],
      "text": "Non-immersive virtual reality (VR) includes specifically gaming applications, such as games on PlayStation, Xbox, or similar systems and games like World of Warcraft or similar games, that rely on computer or video game console displays and input devices such as keyboards, mice, and handheld controllers."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [51, 52, 53, 54, 55],
      "text": "Semi-immersive virtual reality (VR) is in between non-immersive and fully immersive VR, giving the user a partial immersive experience by blocking some but not all senses; for example, the eyes may be blocked while ears and skin remain exposed to real-world signals."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [56, 57, 58, 61, 62],
      "text": "Semi-immersive virtual reality (VR) can be a three-dimensional virtual environment in which users can move by themselves through a computer screen, a VR box, or a VR headset, with a VR headset being the common example; users typically have no real physical movements, and examples include virtual tours using only a headset, which can be device-based or web-based."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [60, 89, 90, 91, 92],
      "text": "Semi-immersive virtual reality (VR) systems are the most cost-effective and commonly used form of VR, and most commercial immersive applications are semi-immersive because they give partial immersion while leaving the user free to sense the real world."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [63, 64, 65, 66, 67, 68, 74, 75],
      "text": "A fully immersive virtual reality (VR) system completely blocks the user's ability to sense the real world by blocking all senses, so the user senses only the things happening in the virtual world, feels physically present in it, and has the user's sense of reality replaced by virtual realism."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [70, 71, 72, 76, 77, 79, 81, 82],
      "text": "Fully immersive virtual reality (VR) systems, such as dedicated virtual shooting game zones, use many devices such as helmets, gloves, body connectors with sense detectors, and body suits, so users view the virtual environment through a helmet or head-mounted display and their actual movements, such as running, jumping, crouching, shooting, and throwing, their reactions, and even a blink of an eye are detected and translated into actions in the virtual world while the user cannot sense anything in the real world."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [69, 83, 84, 85, 86],
      "text": "Among virtual reality (VR) systems, fully immersive systems are the costliest and commercially rare because they are difficult to afford, while semi-immersive and non-immersive systems are more common."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "facts": [
    {
      "evidence": [2, 3, 4],
      "text": "Output devices for virtual reality (VR) experience include visual displays, audio output, haptics, motion platforms and treadmills, and other sensory outputs that stimulate the taste and smell sensory organs, namely the tongue and nose."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [4, 5, 7],
      "text": "Other sensory output systems for taste and smell in virtual reality (VR) are premature, not widely used, available, or popular, and are mostly confined to highly specialized environments or research domains."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [10],
      "text": "Visual displays are displays meant to stimulate the visual sense, that is the eyes."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [11, 12, 13, 14, 15],
      "text": "The three broad types of visual displays are head-mounted displays (HMDs), which are wearable and mobile, world-fixed displays, which are not wearable and are stationary, and regular displays, which are mobile or can be stationary."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [12, 21],
      "text": "Although head-mounted displays (HMDs) can be used as input to track head motion, their primary use is as a display device."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [12, 16, 22, 23],
      "text": "A head-mounted display (HMD) is a sophisticated piece of equipment that integrates electronic, optical, mechanical, and audio components within its package."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [12, 24, 25, 26],
      "text": "A head-mounted display (HMD) places images directly in front of the user's eyes using one or two small screens, with one screen providing monoscopic viewing and two screens providing stereoscopic viewing, and stereoscopic HMDs are more common than monoscopic HMDs."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [12, 27, 28, 29],
      "text": "Head-mounted displays (HMDs) can be further categorized into three types: non-see-through, optical see-through, and video see-through."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [12, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34],
      "text": "Non-see-through HMDs are typically used in virtual reality (VR), optical see-through HMDs are typically used in augmented reality (AR), and video see-through HMDs are typically used in augmented virtuality, in which real-world images or videos appear in real time within an artificial or virtual environment."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [38, 39, 40, 57],
      "text": "A world-fixed display renders graphics onto a surface or screen that is fixed and does not move with the user's head; the display is not moving, while the user can move within a physical space."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [38, 41],
      "text": "World-fixed displays range from standard monitors to displays that completely surround the user, typically called a cave."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [38, 42, 43, 45, 46, 48, 50, 52],
      "text": "The fish tank display, developed in 1993, is an early world-fixed virtual reality (VR) display that uses a fixed cathode-ray tube (CRT) monitor as the output and an optical head tracker and a haptic device as inputs, so that the stereo perspective view of a 3D scene changes as the user's head moves."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [38, 41, 53, 54, 55, 56],
      "text": "A more recent world-fixed display example is the surround screen, or cave system, which typically has three to six large screens around the user for visual immersion and is usually driven by a single or group of powerful graphics engines and requires elaborate tracking of the user's movements."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [14, 58, 59, 60, 61],
      "text": "Regular displays, which can be mobile or stationary, refer to common tablets or smartphones, are not specialized, are typically relevant for non-immersive virtual reality (VR), and are mostly used for augmented reality (AR)."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "facts": [
    {
      "evidence": [1, 2, 3, 4],
      "text": "Cyber sickness can be understood through three aspects: causes, evaluation, and mitigation."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [6, 7, 8],
      "text": "The causes of cyber sickness are broadly internal factors and external factors, and external factors comprise hardware and virtual reality (VR) content."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [11, 13, 14, 43, 75],
      "text": "Internal factors for cyber sickness are individual characteristics and conditions inherent to users that can increase the likelihood of experiencing cyber sickness, including age, gender, motion sickness (MS) susceptibility, physical condition, and emotional condition."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [17, 18, 19, 20, 21, 23, 24],
      "text": "Age is related to cyber sickness susceptibility: children aged 2 to 12 may show increased sensitivity, that tendency decreases as they grow older, older persons are likely to experience higher levels, cyber sickness is generally uncommon among young children, and it is highly unlikely in users aged 12 to 25."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [25, 26],
      "text": "Cyber sickness can be independent of age when users view general virtual reality (VR) content, while age may be a factor for controllable content, making the age relationship clearer for controllable content."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [28, 29, 30, 31, 40, 41],
      "text": "The effect of gender on cyber sickness has no conclusive result because studies report contradictory findings: some find that female users have more severe cases, while others find no discernible difference between male and female participants in the presence or severity of cyber sickness."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [33, 34],
      "text": "Female users who use head-mounted displays (HMDs) are associated with increased cyber sickness."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [33, 34, 35, 36, 38, 39],
      "text": "A possible reason for increased cyber sickness among female head-mounted display (HMD) users is inter-pupillary distance (IPD) fit, because IPD determines stereoscopic vision and a greater proportion of females may have trouble finding a good fit; this implies virtual reality (VR) displays should have a wider IPD adjustable range for female users."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [43, 46, 47],
      "text": "Motion sickness (MS) occurs when the brain receives conflicting signals from various sensory organs, causing symptoms like nausea and dizziness that are similar to cyber sickness."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [48, 49, 50, 51],
      "text": "Virtual reality (VR) recreates realistic movement and visual environments, making the user feel as if in a real-world environment; therefore, users with motion sickness (MS) susceptibility tend also to have cyber sickness susceptibility."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [52, 53],
      "text": "Studies indicate a positive correlation between motion sickness (MS) susceptibility and cyber sickness risk, so individuals with higher MS susceptibility are likely to experience more severe cyber sickness symptoms."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [43, 55, 57, 59, 60],
      "text": "Prior exposure to virtual reality (VR) systems and repeated exposure to VR can reduce the effect of motion sickness (MS) susceptibility on cyber sickness and reduce cyber sickness severity over time, so the positive correlation may not hold if either or both conditions are satisfied."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [62, 63, 64, 65],
      "text": "Body awareness, the ability of the user to attend to and perceive bodily sensations, is a physical condition related to cyber sickness; poor sleep quality can affect this awareness and may contribute to or increase the severity of cyber sickness."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [66],
      "text": "People with greater retinal eccentricity may be more susceptible to cyber sickness."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [67],
      "text": "Individuals with neurological conditions such as multiple sclerosis may experience more severe cyber sickness symptoms."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [68, 69, 70, 71],
      "text": "A user's physical position affects cyber sickness susceptibility: using a virtual reality (VR) system in a standing posture is likely to induce more cyber sickness, or more severe cyber sickness, than using the same system while sitting."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [72, 73, 74],
      "text": "Emotional conditions at the time of use can increase the likelihood of cyber sickness: negative emotions such as anxiety, stress, and frustration are typically associated with it, and personality traits such as neuroticism may also increase the likelihood."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "facts": [
    {
      "evidence": [3, 4, 5],
      "text": "Virtual reality (VR) input devices are broadly divided into hand input devices, which require hands to operate, and non-hand input devices, which do not require hands to operate."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [7, 9, 19, 31, 32, 49, 76],
      "text": "Hand input devices in virtual reality (VR) fall into five broad categories: world-grounded devices, non-tracked handheld controllers, tracked handheld controllers, hand-worn devices, and bare-hand inputs."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [9, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18],
      "text": "World-grounded hand input devices are devices that are fixed in the real world and do not move; a joystick is an example that is commonly used to interact with desktop or other grounded systems, and portability is an issue because they constrain the user to move within a specified area."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [19, 20, 21, 22],
      "text": "Non-tracked handheld controllers are hand input devices that are held in the hand but are not tracked in three-dimensional (3D) space, so the system does not know their exact position with respect to a reference frame; examples include buttons, joysticks, triggers, and traditional video game controllers."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [19, 20, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30],
      "text": "Non-tracked handheld controllers can cause break-in presence because the user's untracked hand movements may not produce a corresponding change in the position of the virtual hands in the virtual world, creating discomfort about whether the user is performing the task appropriately and affecting the perception of presence in the virtual world."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [31, 32, 36, 37, 38, 39, 40, 41],
      "text": "Tracked handheld controllers are hand input devices with six degrees of freedom tracking, so the system can track their movements; input controllers that come with modern virtual reality (VR) systems are tracked devices so that break-in presence is avoided, and VIVE controllers are an example because they combine a joystick with tracking and are mainly used in VR."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [31, 32, 44, 45, 46, 47, 48],
      "text": "Tracked handheld controllers have the limitation that users cannot directly or fully touch and feel passive virtual objects through tangible interaction because the user's hands are occupied with the controller, although force feedback controllers may sometimes resolve the issue."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [49, 50, 51, 52],
      "text": "Hand-worn hand input devices are devices that are worn on the hands, arms, or fingers; examples include data gloves, electromyography (EMG) bands, and rings, with data gloves being more popular."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [53, 54, 55, 57, 58, 59],
      "text": "Bend-sensing data gloves are data gloves with sensors at all bend positions or joints and other positions; their processing unit receives continuous raw data from these bend sensors and processes it to detect hand postures and gestures, and because their many sensors make any gesture likely to generate a different data stream for unambiguous interpretation, they provide a large degree of freedom that can make interaction more natural than controller-based interaction."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [60, 61, 62, 63, 64, 65],
      "text": "Pinch gloves are data gloves in which the sensors are placed only at fingertip positions, which may vary but often include the thumb and index finger fingertips; they detect a limited set of pinch gestures, are good at determining whether the fingertips are touching, and are typically used for discrete input such as object selection and mode switching."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [49, 67, 68, 69, 70, 71],
      "text": "The advantages of hand-worn hand input devices are natural input with potentially rich gesture interaction, comfortable hand positions, no line-of-sight issue unlike some controller-based input, and the ability of the user's hands and fingers to fully interact with real objects, making different sorts of perception possible."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [49, 72, 73, 74],
      "text": "Issues with hand-worn hand input devices are that recognizing many gestures requires users to recalibrate often or to learn and remember many gestures, which can create problems for different user categories, and that long exposure time can become uncomfortable because the hands may become sweaty."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [76, 77, 78, 79, 80, 81, 82, 83],
      "text": "Bare-hand inputs are the most natural hand input interactions: the system tracks the user's bare hand using computer vision techniques, the user does not need to wear anything or use any external device, and computer vision algorithms detect the user's gestures and translate them into commands in the virtual environment, leaving the user's hands completely free."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [76, 84, 85, 88, 89],
      "text": "The Leap Motion device is an example of a bare-hand input device: it is an infrared (IR)-based hand-tracking sensor that typically contains three IR light-emitting diodes (LEDs) and two wide-angle cameras; the LEDs generate IR light reflected from the hands and picked up by the cameras, and software converts the signals into gestures."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [84, 86, 87, 91, 92, 93, 94],
      "text": "The Leap Motion device can be attached to a head-mounted display (HMD) to provide hand input support even if the HMD does not support hand input, and it is typically useful with low-cost HMDs that do not provide good-quality controllers or controller support, such as cardboard-type devices in which a smartphone provides a stereoscopic view and which do not support interaction."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [84, 95, 97, 98, 99],
      "text": "The Leap Motion device has problems: fatigue may occur from holding hands in front of its sensor or camera, the hand must be in the line of sight of its camera, and consistent recognition of gestures across a wide range of users may be an issue."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "facts": [
    {
      "evidence": [2, 3, 30, 37, 42, 48, 70],
      "text": "Developer-side strategies for reducing cybersickness (CS) include field of view (FOV) adjustment, dynamic visual blur, rest frames, optical-flow manipulation, hardware-level alterations, and locomotion modifications."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [3, 4],
      "text": "Field of view (FOV) is the amount of virtual environment that is visible at any moment."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [5, 6, 8],
      "text": "Field of view (FOV) adjustment can, ideally, mitigate the onset or severity of cybersickness (CS) because restricting the field of view reduces peripheral optical flow."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [9],
      "text": "Field of view (FOV) adjustment has been found to be effective across genders without any negative impacts."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [10, 11],
      "text": "A drawback of field of view (FOV) adjustment is that it can reduce presence or sense of presence and impair performance in visual tasks because it restricts certain visual content, potentially degrading user experience."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [14, 15, 16],
      "text": "A symmetric mask is a classical field of view (FOV) adjustment technique that occludes the periphery in reaction to translational and/or angular velocity, but it can affect presence."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [17, 18, 19, 20, 21],
      "text": "An asymmetric mask is a field of view (FOV) adjustment strategy that is considered an adaptive restrictor because it obscures peripheral regions with higher optical flow during virtual locomotion while leaving regions with lower optical flow visible, so it adapts based on optical flow."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [22, 23, 24, 25],
      "text": "A side restrictor, described as an asymmetric mask, is a field of view (FOV) adjustment strategy that obscures only one side of the periphery during virtual rotation and laterally shifts the center of restriction in the direction of the turn, providing a controlled way of restriction."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [26, 27],
      "text": "A ground visible restrictor is a field of view (FOV) adjustment strategy in which the ground plane remains visible during movement."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [30, 31, 32, 33, 34, 35, 36],
      "text": "Dynamic visual blur is a technique for reducing the possibility of cybersickness (CS) in which blurring means obscuring or reducing clarity, and it has two types: depth-of-field blur, which uses well-known depth cues in human vision, and peripheral blur, also called dynamic non-salient region blurring."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [37, 38, 39, 40],
      "text": "Rest frames are fixed elements within the virtual environment that remain stationary relative to the real world regardless of the user's movements, and rest frames attached to the head reference frame are known as independent visual backgrounds, with examples including clouds, grids, and a virtual nose."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [42, 43, 45, 46, 47],
      "text": "Optical-flow manipulation is a cybersickness (CS) reduction technique that can involve visually mixing optical-flow patterns in the reverse direction of virtual visual motion, using a restricted field of view (FOV) aligned with optical flow, or reducing perceived optical flow by geometrical simplification of the virtual environment."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [48, 49, 50],
      "text": "Hardware-level alterations can reduce or eliminate cybersickness (CS) by modifying existing hardware to different degrees or by proposing completely new hardware."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [48, 51, 52, 53, 54],
      "text": "Hardware-level alterations for reducing cybersickness (CS) include a sparse peripheral display that expands a head-mounted display (HMD) periphery with a colorful LED matrix, and miniature LED screens placed in the HMD."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [48, 55, 56],
      "text": "Hardware-level alterations for reducing cybersickness (CS) include a Noble camera setup twin cam that reduces motion blur and cybersickness, and a hybrid 360 panoramic video camera rig that can be used to reduce cybersickness."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [48, 57, 58, 59],
      "text": "Phantom legs is a hardware-level alteration that applies alternating haptic cues synchronized to users' footsteps using additional hardware, and it was found to reduce cybersickness (CS) to a great extent."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [48, 60, 61],
      "text": "Floor vibrations are a hardware-level alteration for reducing cybersickness (CS) that uses vibration-driven whole-body tactile feedback to match the vibrations of the virtual environment."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [48, 63, 64, 65],
      "text": "Simultaneous presentation of sound and vibration is a hardware-level alteration that has been tried for reducing cybersickness (CS), for example in a walking vibe system that uses two small vibration motors behind the ears for vibrotactile feedback during a walking experience."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [48, 66, 67, 68],
      "text": "Modified motion platforms are a hardware-level alteration in which short bursts of rotational acceleration matching visual forward acceleration were found quite effective in reducing cybersickness (CS), and the platforms can be modified to produce this effect."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [70, 72],
      "text": "Locomotion techniques can play a crucial role in preventing cybersickness (CS), and they can be modified or new techniques can be proposed to reduce its effect."
