      "evidence": [1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 10, 11],
      "text": "In the calibration of inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensors, a gyroscope provides angular velocity, and the measured angular velocity (omega_g) can differ from the actual angular velocity (omega_a) because the devices and measurement technique are imperfect, so the measurement error is omega_g minus omega_a."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 2, 6, 13, 26],
      "text": "In the calibration stage for inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensors, the objective is to make the measured angular velocity (omega_g) and the actual angular velocity (omega_a) as close as possible, which means reducing the measurement error to as small a value as possible."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [2, 6, 14, 16, 18, 20, 21, 22],
      "text": "One simple calibration model assumes a linear relationship between measured angular velocity (omega_g) and actual angular velocity (omega_a), for example omega_g = a + b omega_a, where the ideal condition omega_g = omega_a corresponds to a = 0 and b = 1, but this ideal condition never happens in practice and the linear relationship is not the only possible relationship."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 2, 6, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 36],
      "text": "The presented calibration uses many, sometimes thousands, paired samples from two sensors: a cheap, inaccurate inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor that produces the measured values (omega_g), and an expensive, highly accurate benchmark sensor that produces the actual values (omega_a), although the benchmark is very close rather than exact or ideal."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [2, 6, 33, 35, 36, 37, 38, 39, 40, 41, 42, 43],
      "text": "A simple calibration method compares the paired samples by computing, for n pairs, the sum of the squared differences between the expression C1 + C2 omega_g and the actual values omega_a, and it finds the constants C1 and C2 that minimize this sum; this classical optimization is linear least squares regression, one of many techniques that is used here for simplicity."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [2, 6, 38, 44, 45, 46, 47],
      "text": "After the constants C1 and C2 are determined, calibration mathematically transforms the cheap sensor reading into a calibrated value omega_cal, defined as omega_cal = C1 + C2 omega_g, mapping the inexpensive measured angular velocity omega_g to the closest possible match with the actual value rather than by using an actually expensive or highly accurate sensor."
    }
  ]
}

