      "evidence": [5, 6, 7, 8, 9],
      "text": "A matched zone is a place where the real world and the virtual world perfectly align; it corresponds to the safe real-world region where a user's physical movement is restricted, although the virtual world is not similarly confined, and movement within that region is translated to movement in other parts of the virtual world."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [10, 11, 12, 14, 16],
      "text": "In an immersive virtual reality (VR) application that is not carefully implemented, an obstacle mismatch can occur when a virtual obstacle blocks the user without a corresponding physical obstacle in the matched zone, or when a physical obstacle exists in the matched zone without a corresponding virtual obstacle."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [19, 20, 22, 23, 24],
      "text": "In virtual reality (VR), the real-world motions reflected in the virtual world vary among virtual reality experiences: head motions must be matched so the visual renderer uses the correct viewpoint, and other body parts require tracking and matched motion when the user needs hand-eye coordination or looks at those body parts during the experience."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [17, 18, 32, 33],
      "text": "Virtual reality (VR) software should include developer support for resolving matched-motion challenges, such as deciding which real-world motions to reflect and creating a correspondence between a user's physical motion in the matched zone and a different virtual motion."
