      "evidence": [16, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24],
      "text": "George Miller's 1956 psychological finding, the seven plus or minus two rule, gives the short-term memory limit as five to nine chunks of information at a time, so holding fewer underutilizes short-term memory and holding more overutilizes it and is harmful."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [14, 28, 29, 30],
      "text": "The last usability rule, reducing short-term memory load, requires keeping the information needed to operate an interface or perform an interaction between five and nine chunks for the user's optimum outcome, and not forcing users to remember more than nine chunks."
