{
  "facts": [
    {
      "evidence": [1, 2, 6, 7],
      "text": "Usability requirements for the Majuli virtual reality (VR) Tour were gathered by making field trips to Majuli, interviewing people, observing people in their natural work settings, and collecting data in the form of notes."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 8, 9, 10, 12],
      "text": "The Majuli virtual reality (VR) Tour is intended for people who do not reside on Majuli rather than residents, because residents are least likely to use it since they are already on the island."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19],
      "text": "Three potential user groups in the Majuli virtual reality (VR) Tour case study were identified through field trips, interviews, observations, and local people's experience of typical visitors and their activities: students or learners interested in knowing about the island, tourists interested in exploring tourist spots or other interesting places on the island, and pilgrims interested in religious places and practices."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 21, 22, 23],
      "text": "The usability of the Majuli virtual reality (VR) Tour is claimed only for the three identified user groups—students or learners, tourists, and pilgrims—and it is not claimed to be usable for any person in any work context or task."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 13, 24, 55],
      "text": "Contextual inquiry (CI) in the Majuli virtual reality (VR) Tour case study was conducted after the user groups were identified, on samples from those user groups, to learn their needs and expectations, identify the system features to be supported for each group, and identify specific issues for those groups related to broad design challenges."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 28, 29, 30],
      "text": "A key design challenge in the Majuli virtual reality (VR) Tour case study was to provide a comprehensive view of a large geographical area with large multimodal data while avoiding cyber sickness, because exploring the entire island with all collected data is time consuming and may cause cyber sickness."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 13, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34, 35, 36],
      "text": "The large geographical area and cyber sickness challenge in the Majuli virtual reality (VR) Tour case study was addressed by allowing each user group to access only a subset of the collected data, with each subset identified through contextual inquiry (CI) based on what that group typically does."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [1, 13, 32, 37, 38, 39, 40, 41],
      "text": "The data-subset restriction in the Majuli virtual reality (VR) Tour case study reduced the expanse of information and the need to explore everything in detail, reduced the possibility of cyber sickness, and increased interest for each group, whereas making the entire data set accessible to all users could confuse users, reduce their interest, and lead to cyber sickness."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [42, 43, 44],
      "text": "Accurate location information in any virtual reality (VR) tour is crucial, especially for large geographies, because it prevents disorientation and gives users more confidence and purpose by telling them where they are in the geographical space."
    },
    {
