    {
      "evidence": [2, 26, 28, 29, 30, 31],
      "text": "Quick or expert evaluation can rely on design-team members or domain experts rather than end users because they are assumed to have user-like experience, their feedback likely reflects users, and engaging them is less complicated, quicker, and cheaper than engaging a full evaluation team with end users."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [2, 32, 33],
      "text": "The evaluation team for expert evaluation should have at least three to five members; more members are welcome, but three to five is sufficient."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [2, 34, 35, 36, 37, 38],
      "text": "In expert evaluation, each evaluator is presented with the prototype, evaluates it independently, and produces a report containing the usability issues perceived by that evaluator; the design team or team lead then combines these reports into a final list of usability issues."
