}

{
  "facts": [
    {
      "evidence": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],
      "text": "Cognitive walkthrough is a quick expert evaluation technique and usability inspection method in which usability problems are identified by inspecting a prototype."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [6, 7, 8, 9, 20],
      "text": "A cognitive walkthrough requires at least a low-fidelity vertical prototype, a prototype that supports several tasks and may be low- or medium-fidelity, and an evaluation team of three to five members; a high-fidelity prototype is more welcome but involves more time and cost."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [20, 21, 22, 23],
      "text": "A low-fidelity prototype for a cognitive walkthrough can be made as simple paper prototypes using the storyboarding approach, in which each prototype is a series of sketches depicting screen changes after each interaction, that is, a storyboard."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [24, 31, 33, 34, 35, 37, 38],
      "text": "In a cognitive walkthrough, a scenario or task description is created for the evaluators, and the task described in the scenario is converted into interface-level tasks, which are the actions that must be performed in the prototype to accomplish that task; the evaluators then perform those actions with the prototype."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [40, 41, 49, 50],
      "text": "Usability questions are framed before a cognitive walkthrough to reflect usability issues, and evaluators use them as guidelines to identify problems users are likely to face while performing the tasks."
    },
    {
      "evidence": [51, 52, 53, 54],
      "text": "For each usability question, evaluators must give a detailed report rather than a simple yes or no answer, explaining where they felt difficulty in the interface or interaction and why."
    },
